# `__modname__` command group

`weld add module __modname__ --command` generated this package. weld writes it
once and records every file in `weld.json`; it does not regenerate them, so your
edits survive later `weld add` commands.

## What it is

A command group backed by the `internal/modules/__modname__` business module: it
receives the same `Service` interface and `NewService` constructor the HTTP
handler uses, so the command and `GET /api/__modname__` share one business layer.
A bare `__name__ __modname__` shows help; the generated `greet` subcommand is a
focused example of the Service seam, and `status` is the worked example of a
subcommand that reads a second injected dependency.

## How it is wired

Generating the module command installed `http` and `loom` (and the `config` they
require). The group resolves its dependencies from the command-specific graph in
`internal/di/__modname___graph.go`. That graph binds `__modname__.NewService`
(the same factory the HTTP server graph binds) and includes `commonModule`, the
shared provider set `di.go` declares (configuration, logger and the installed
db/redis/mail/storage bindings); Loom prunes every provider a subcommand does not
consume, so the group never constructs the HTTP server, the scheduler or a
database pool. A capability installed after this command still reaches it,
because the shared providers live in `commonModule` rather than in this stable
graph.

`internal/app/__modname___command.go` registers the group through
`app.RegisterCommand` and delegates `Build` to `di.Init__ModName__`. Each
subcommand calls `commandkit.Run`, which builds and starts the graph for that one
invocation and stops it within a bounded deadline. Keep the module's `Service`
interface and `NewService` symbol stable; `internal/di/loom_gen.go` is generated
by Loom and must never be edited.

## Injecting a dependency

`Deps` is the root of the command's graph: one field per dependency a subcommand
needs, and one matching parameter on `NewDeps`. `Service` is the module's
business layer; `Logger` is the worked example of a second field. There are two
cases:

- A dependency the shared `commonModule` already provides needs nothing else —
  `*config.Config`, `*slog.Logger` and, when installed, `data.Repository` and the
  Redis, mail and storage bindings. Adding the field and the `NewDeps` parameter
  is all it takes, and Loom injects it.
- A dependency no provider declares needs a provider in
  `internal/di/__modname___graph.go`, for example `loom.Provide(NewThing)`, with
  the same field here and the same parameter on `NewDeps`.

A capability installed after this command still reaches it, because the shared
providers live in `commonModule`. `internal/commandkit` is the shared lazy-build
seam, so the command package never imports Loom itself.
